PRE-TABLE AMENDMENT, PART 7b -- its own file, written AFTER part 7 and BEFORE part 7b.

SECTION 15 -- a prediction that FAILED in the loud direction, and the mutant it mis-named.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Section 14 predicted M_IDATA_NE1_ALL (`len != 1' -> `idata != 1' at all twelve guards) ->
170/171 with `latch all one' red.  MEASURED (part7/driver.log): 95/171, seventy-six rows red --
val h genctl, val w sccerr, every rb, rb2, rb4, rb8 ... -- because the bare swap DROPS THE
LENGTH TEST: a byte write whose value is not 1 is declined too, so the presets never land.
The finding (Opus F-K) named that swap; the shape it MEANT, and the shape `latch all one'
exists for, is the value-test QUALIFIER `len != 1 && idata != 1 && writeflag == MEM_WRITE':
wide writes of the value 1 land, everything else behaves as shipped, and before `latch all
one' no row wide-wrote 1.  The contract's C-warrant sentence and the pass-7 CHANGELOG line
named the swap form; both now name the qualifier and record the swap's measured kill (L4).
A misprediction in the loud direction costs nothing but a record; the same class in the safe
direction (Section 13) cost two vacuous rows.
PREDICTIONS for part 7b (one mutant plus BASE, the 171-row instrument as now shipped):
    BASE                171/171
    M_IDATA_NE1_QUAL    170/171   latch all one (DECL 0: all twelve value-1 writes land); every
                                  other row green (no other row wide-writes the value 1)
